   In {{$.Integration}}, each pull request's environment signs in through the same
   client: add its callback,
   `https://{{$.App}}-pr<N>.{{$.P.AppsDomain}}{{.Callback.Path}}`, to the client's
   authorized redirect URIs before signing in there, until the organization
   runs a test identity gateway.
{{- if .Groups}}
   The role-groups read needs no step of its own: the sign-in reads a person's
   groups through the Cloud Identity Groups API with their own sign-in token,
   and 1-org enables that API in the environment project. Google charges a
   call made with a person's token to the project that owns the OAuth client,
   not to the one the application runs in, which is why the client belongs in
   the environment project; a client created in any other project needs the
   Cloud Identity API enabled there too, or every sign-in fails at the
   callback (a 403 from Google, while the screen blames the application). The
   groups themselves are made in the Workspace Admin console, each set so that
   its members can view its member list (the infrastructure repository's
   README, "The role groups"). If the role groups
   are nested, set `{{.Var "GROUP_LOOKUP"}} = "nested"` in `terraform.tfvars`.
{{- end}}
{{end -}}
{{end -}}
{{range .Administrators -}}
{{.Step}}. Name {{.Label}}'s first administrator in `terraform.tfvars` (`{{.Var}}`), per
   environment: the sign-in name the directory presents (the user principal name
   on Entra, the email on Google). The migrate command of the first release in the
   environment seeds the administrator role membership under it while the auth's
   users table holds no rows, and refuses to seed while the name is empty; the
   person then signs in through the directory and holds the role.
{{end -}}
{{if .Mailbox -}}
{{.MailboxStep}}. In the Google Workspace Admin console, once for the organization: a mailbox the
   application sends its mail as (a no-reply address of the organization's domain{{if .MailFrom}},
   or a real account with the no-reply address as one of its aliases, since an alias
   cannot log in while the relay accepts it as the From address{{end}}),
   with 2-step verification turned on and an app password generated for it, and the
   SMTP relay service (Apps > Google Workspace > Gmail > Routing) accepting mail from
   the organization's addresses with authentication and TLS required. Put the mailbox
   in `terraform.tfvars` (`mail_mailbox`{{if .MailFrom}}, and the alias in `mail_from` when the
   account is not the no-reply address itself{{end}}){{if .MailAppPassword}} and add the app password as version 1 of
   `{{$.Prefix}}-<env>-gbl-{{$.App}}-{{.MailAppPassword.Name}}` in every environment: the password is a
   declared secret, so a release to an environment after {{$.Integration}} waits on its
   pin{{end}}. Until the mailbox is named the application writes its mail (the
   invitations and address verifications its auths send, link included) to the log
   and sends nothing, which a deployed environment is not left at.
{{end -}}
{{if not .CookieKeySecret.Generated -}}
{{.CookieKeyStep}}. Generate a cookie key (`openssl rand -base64 32`) and add it as version 1
   of `{{.Prefix}}-<env>-gbl-{{.App}}-{{.CookieKeySecret.Name}}`.
{{end -}}
{{if .PinnedSecrets -}}
{{.PinStep}}. Pin the versions in `secret_versions` and apply.
{{end -}}
{{if not .HandSteps -}}
None: every secret the code declares is generated (the first release build in
each environment adds its first version and pins it; the first apply mounts
none yet), and the sign-in needs no registration.
{{end}}
